\label{264271}
Assume $A\in\mathbb{Q}^{n\times n}$ is skew-Hermitian. Then there is a unitary quaternion matrix $U$ and a diagonal matrix $\Lambda$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{88}
UAU^{\ast}=\Lambda=\mathrm{diag}(r_{1}I_{k_{1}}\boldsymbol{i},r_{2}I_{k_{2}}\boldsymbol{i},\ldots,r_{r}I_{k_{r}}\boldsymbol{i}),
\end{equation}
where $0\leq r_{1}<\cdots<r_{r}$, $I_{k_{j}}$ is a $k_{j}\times k_{j}$ identity matrix and $\sum_{j=1}^{r}k_{j}=n$.

\label{5}
Assume $\phi\in\mathbb{S}$ and $B\in\mathbb{DQ}^{n\times n}$ is $\phi$-Hermitian. Then there exists a unitary matrix $V\in\mathbb{DQ}^{n\times n}$ and a diagonal matrix $\Gamma$ such that
\begin{equation}\label{13}
VBV^{*{\phi}}=\Gamma=\mathrm{diag}(r_{1}+R_{11}\epsilon,\ldots,r_{1}+R_{1m_{1}}\epsilon,r_{2}+R_{21}\epsilon,\ldots,r_{r}+R_{rm_{r}}\epsilon),
\end{equation}
where 
$$
0\leq r_{1}<\cdots<r_{r},\ \ R_{i1}\leq\cdots\leq R_{im_{i}},\ \ R_{ij}\in\mathbb{R},\ \ \sum_{i=1}^{r}m_{i}=n.
$$
